This paper delves into the integration of conditional Wasserstein GANs and Bayesian imaging models through optimization, resulting in a novel tool for posterior expansion. By leveraging adversarial principles and Bayesian statistics, we can significantly broaden the scope of feasible solutions by incorporating prior information. Nonetheless, the approach is impeded by the challenge of designing a discriminator that assesses distance in a complex data space. To overcome this limitation, we propose a simplified formulation based on an altered distance metric, one that is manageable for both computational purposes and mathematical analysis. A central aspect of this work is the demonstration of a controller's existence and continuity, both theoretically and through numerical validation, in addition to its beneficial consequences for enhancing deconvolution image reconstruction. The proposed method was tested on a range-contrast source identification problem with notable results. Furthermore, we found that the adversarial approach outperforms traditional Tikhonov regularization techniques. To facilitate replication of our findings, the code has been made openly accessible.... 

